PIERRE ETTORI: Good
afternoon, and thanks

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:08.800
for joining us for
this presentation.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.170
Today we are going
to be presenting

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:15.520
how Google is helping companies
to revolutionize how they

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.610
consume and deliver
application in the cloud

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:22.190
by enabling a service-centric
approach to networking.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:25.060
We will be presenting how
Private Service Connect

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.940
and Service Directory allow
us to connect seamlessly

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.420
and privately to services
and manage them at scale.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:33.920
My name is Pierre
Ettori, and I'm

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.140
a product manager at Google
Cloud and Private Service

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.510
Connect, and with me is Matt.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.368
So from an agena
point of view, first

00:00:40.368 --> 00:00:42.410
we will be looking at the
challenges of consuming

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.050
services at scale
[INAUDIBLE] to talking

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.240
about this service-centric
networking approach

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.780
that we will be leading and
introducing Private Service

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.640
Connect and Service Directory.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.280
And finally, we'll be bringing
Private Service Connect

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.140
and Service Directory
together and see

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.290
how they are better together.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.349
Business leaders
across industries

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:08.410
are prioritizing growth,
innovation, and time to market.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:12.390
In fact, in a recent
study, 94% of the CEOs

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.080
indicated that at least two
of the top three business

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.170
priorities depend on how well
technology team are executing.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.970
So the responsibility to
deliver services faster

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:28.310
is increasingly being
placed on their shoulders.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.430
This is a huge
responsibility that

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:33.310
needs to be met with the
right tools and software

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.700
for them to be successful.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.420
Many CIOs acknowledge that
migrating to the cloud,

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.180
adopting new application
architecture,

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.370
and building cloud
native services

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:50.330
will help them jump start growth
and accelerate them to market.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:54.580
However, 80% of them report
that despite having migrated

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.680
a portion of their
application, they

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.400
have struggled to achieve
the desired level of agility

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.490
and business benefits.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.830
In conversation with
technology leaders,

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.830
three main points come
up again and again.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.670
Number one, complexity and
slow speed of migration.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.580
Number two, they
want to modernize now

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.140
so they can increase efficiency
without business disruption.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:21.060
And number three, they
want to consume services

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.730
faster and focus on
value-added services.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:29.570
So companies are trying to
consume more and more services

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:33.347
faster so they can deliver
on their business priorities.

00:02:33.347 --> 00:02:35.180
But as we have seen,
many of those companies

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.040
are also admitting
that they haven't

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.980
been able to completely
unlock the full potential

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.710
that the service
consumption can wreak.

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.620
One of the reason is that
consuming services securely

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.790
and at scale exposes a lot of
complexity on how we connect

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:51.690
to them and manage them.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.630
And we're going to look
at it first in terms

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.510
of connecting to those
services and then

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:00.290
on how to manage the application
and services at scale.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.630
And connecting to services
at scale is complex today.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.150
Cloud and network
architects had to do

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.160
a lot of preplanning
and engineering

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.710
to expose services privately.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.050
They typically had to
exchange infrastructure

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.540
global information,
like IP addresses

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.330
and subnet coordination with
technology like DPC pairing,

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.600
so different domain
could communicate.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.190
Or they had to manage
complex routing topology.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.100
They also had to do that
across different organization,

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:31.970
networks, accounts, making
things a lot more complicated,

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.060
especially for regulatory
reasons or security

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:38.870
best practices, the enterprise
wants to keep the services

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:40.880
completely protected
from internet

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.620
and only use private
IP addresses.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:48.010
Finally, it is not only
about how to consume services

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.590
but also how to
deliver services.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:53.680
And we are seeing an explosion
of different type of services.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.380
Some are single-tenant,
some are multitenant,

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:59.350
some are on-demand, and
some are pre-established.

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.500
So connecting all
those services at scale

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.380
securely and privately
while enabling innovation

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:08.920
in simplicity is really complex.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.160
And the other complexity,
slowing down companies

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.710
to deliver on agility
and faster time to market

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.350
is managing services at scale.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.300
As more and more
teams and companies

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.579
are consuming and
delivering services,

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:22.890
we have seen an
explosion of technology,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.380
focusing on service
consumption from cloud

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.560
and SaaS vendor or
new services framework

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.220
like microservices architecture.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:32.950
And it is all for the best.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:34.800
However, it came at
the cost of having

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.030
to manage all services at scale,
especially in this world which

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:43.160
is multi-everything, like
multi-cloud, multi-environment,

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.615
and multi-environments we
took about on-prem or GKE

00:04:45.615 --> 00:04:47.820
or serverless function.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.990
Well, we are also multi-cluster
and multi-languages.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.150
So the explosion of services,
framework, and platform

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.580
is making the discovery and
publishing of those services

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.800
complex, especially
because it needs

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.470
to be done in a way that is
not stifling the creation

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.330
and growth of those services.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.220
Teams should be
evolving independently,

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.590
choosing the best platform
with delivery mechanism

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.160
for their needs, but really
leaving DevOps or cloud team

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.090
with the really complex task to
enable this exponential growth,

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:19.080
while at the same time keeping
system in control and secure.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.600
And finally, as we have seen,
if the cloud engineering

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.840
team ends up spending a
significant amount of time

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.770
mastering complexity
to managing services.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.580
As the scale increase,
it becomes harder

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.410
to stay as productive.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:35.130
And yet we are putting on
their shoulder a lot more

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.350
responsibility,
the responsibility

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.790
to deliver those key services.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.970
So we need to make sure
the cloud engineering

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:45.930
team can stay focused and
productive on value-added task.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.230
So mastering complexity
versus extracting simplicity

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.540
means being able to
rethink the primitives

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.790
that one exposes
to actually truly

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.640
empower the agility and
velocity organization they're

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.390
working for.

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:01.530
And extracting simplicity
means rethinking

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:04.890
the complexity exposed from
the underlying platform.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:06.810
And many of our
technology have evolved,

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.160
starting with infrastructure.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.880
And so a lot of the
complexity come from that--

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.800
come from those
system that are still

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.770
exposing infrastructure-centric
networking,

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.680
instead of focusing
on services delivery.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.450
And so at Google,
we are delivering

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:23.430
a service-centric
networking approach

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.360
instead of the
infrastructure-centric

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.190
so enterprises and end user can
focus on connecting, consuming,

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:34.330
delivering, and managing
services securely at scale.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.460
Services-centric
networking solution

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:38.920
focus on services
connectivity and management,

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.230
without having to
worry on the complexity

00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:43.390
of the underlying
infrastructure.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:47.230
Organization can finally execute
on the [INAUDIBLE] innovation

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:50.800
and enable technology teams to
deliver and consume services.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.680
Since the underlying
infrastructure is not exposed,

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.540
the connectivity and
management of services

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.120
become much simpler,
secure, and private.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:00.310
It accelerate the
migration to cloud,

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:03.230
since companies can connect
simply from on-prem to new

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.670
services in the cloud faster,
while enforcing the security

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:08.410
standard and best practices--

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:11.050
for example, leveraging
private IP space.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:13.460
Finally, by simplifying
the management of services

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.640
at scale, it enable
enduring team

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.010
to execute and innovate faster.

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:20.110
And so with that, we
are excited to present

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:23.110
our service-centric networking
product, Private Service

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:27.370
Connect, to connect privately to
services, and Service Directory

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:31.792
to manage and then reduce
their services at scale.

00:07:31.792 --> 00:07:34.250
As you may have seen during
the keynote from earlier today,

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:37.020
we are excited to present to
you Private Service Connect.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.080
Private Service Connect allows
to connect and consume services

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.410
easily and privately to many
different type of services.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.750
By focusing on
service-centric networking,

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.310
Private Service Connect abstract
the underlying infrastructure

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:53.310
for both the team consuming
and delivering services,

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.740
which greatly simplifies
how they connect

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:57.240
and access services.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.670
It is doing all of that
using private connectivity,

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:03.520
meaning the traffic is never
exposed to the public internet.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.260
And finally, it drastically
simplified the communication

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:09.672
and upfront planning necessary
to build network architecture

00:08:09.672 --> 00:08:11.130
that used to happen
with technology

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:14.200
like [? VPC ?] pairing.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:17.240
So Private Service Connect
offer us many advantages,

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:18.240
and we can--

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.310
we're going to look at that
in more deeper details.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:26.330
First, Private Service Connect
enable to consume services

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:31.580
faster, meaning on one
side, consumer of services

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.960
can operate independently
from producer of services.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:40.039
They don't need to pre-agree
on the routing or subnets or IP

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:41.450
information.

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:44.690
And we support any
type of tenancy

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:47.520
for the delivery of the
services, can be single-tenant

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.850
or ridge multi-tenant services.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:52.880
Private Service Connect really
enabled a private and seamless

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.560
connectivity.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.050
For consumer, it becomes
really easy for them

00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:59.450
to [INAUDIBLE] to those
services because they just

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:02.240
need to create an
endpoint on their network.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.910
It feel it belongs to them, and
that's the only configuration

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:05.660
they have to do.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:08.720
They don't need to talk more
to the person delivering

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:09.530
that series.

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:13.260
They just create an endpoint
and now can connect to it.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:15.830
And as we have seen,
it's private end-to-end

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:16.880
connectivity.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.310
The traffic never has
to go in internet.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:22.120
It is all secure, meaning
the companies tenders,

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:26.550
so security's tenders, that are
so important to move to cloud.

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:30.050
And finally, it really
accelerate cloud consumption

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:34.010
because it enabled to support,
really, any type of services.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.350
Could be first party
Google services,

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:38.470
like Google Services
at Google API.

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:40.820
It would be third-party
partners or vendors

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.710
offering the services.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:45.710
And it would be also the
customer-owned services

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:47.750
that they are
delivering internally,

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:53.000
making that communication
faster and the delivery better.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.790
Private Service Connect
has been build and designed

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:58.550
for security, scale,
and performance.

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:01.310
Security, as we have been
talking over and over,

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:03.590
is a big, big theme
for enterprises

00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:05.480
to be able to move to cloud.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:07.280
And especially as
[INAUDIBLE] consume

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.410
and we consume more
and more services that

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:12.660
are production-ready,
you want security.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.860
You want to feel that
that traffic is secure.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.780
And with Private
Service Connect,

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:22.150
all the traffic stays on Google
backbone and is encrypted.

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:26.360
And it is leveraging the IP
addresses of both the consumer

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.420
and the producer, really
feeling that they are leveraging

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.880
the private IP spaces.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.570
It's scaled for
enterprise grade solution,

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:39.650
meaning we can support thousands
of Private Service Connection

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:44.120
to services, greatly, greatly
simplifying the type of designs

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:47.360
where scalability
and quotas or things

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:49.220
like that were a concern.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:51.290
And finally, in
terms of performance,

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:53.480
Private Service
Connect has been build

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:56.750
with no proxy or
middle boxes as part

00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:00.830
of the solution it is truly
line rate, really allowing

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:04.160
the consumer and the producer
to access each service as if it

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.640
was VM-to-VM traffic,
and so really thinking

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:11.480
in terms of performance here
so we can support, really,

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:14.580
as many type of
services and performance

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:15.455
[INAUDIBLE] services.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.660
So if we look a little bit in
more detail of Private Service

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:23.587
Connect, you will
see how simple it is.

00:11:23.587 --> 00:11:26.170
We have talked so far about the
consumer side and the producer

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:28.550
side, and we're going
to reuse that here.

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:30.560
If we look first on
the right-hand side

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:33.890
of the slide with a
producer, a producer

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:36.920
is offering a service
behind the load balancer.

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:39.170
That service could be
any type of services.

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:40.910
The only thing that
the producer needs

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:43.550
to do to enable
Private Service Connect

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:45.800
is to create a
service attachment.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.910
And a service attachment
is a new type of resource

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:48.990
that's really simple.

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:50.370
It just has a name.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.520
It has an IP from
the producer network,

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:54.977
so it belongs on that IP space.

00:11:54.977 --> 00:11:57.060
And we have built a lot
of [INAUDIBLE] information

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.420
in governance to make
sure that the connectivity

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:01.940
to that service is
going to be secure

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.036
and following the
company best practices.

00:12:05.036 --> 00:12:08.230
On the consumer side, on
the left side, the really--

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:09.670
it becomes really
simple for them

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:12.970
to consume a service because the
only thing that they have to do

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:16.070
is actually create an endpoint
that would have a target.

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:19.480
And the target would be
that service attachment

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:21.220
and on top of
that, it would have

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:25.230
an IP address from
the consumer IP space,

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:29.620
again, highlighting how
consumer and producer don't

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:32.950
need to really pre-communicate
or pre-agree on anything.

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:36.160
The consumer use whatever
is its own IP space,

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:39.350
and the producer use
whatever is its own IP space.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:41.410
And there is no
coordination needed

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.980
to connect a consumer and a
producer service together.

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:49.250
So really, as we have
seen, for companies

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:53.000
to fully take advantage of the
desired level of agility that

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:53.870
cloud--

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:56.630
consuming new services and
building cloud-native services

00:12:56.630 --> 00:13:00.110
promised, we need simple
and scalable solution

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.890
for connecting and
managing those services.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:06.590
Private Service Connect focus
on the connectivity aspect

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:09.410
and enables seamless and
private service consumption

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.570
to Google Services,
third-party vendor,

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:13.330
or customer-owned services.

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:15.990
Their traffic is never
exposed to the internet,

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:17.960
and it has the same
performance characteristic

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:19.970
of that VM-to-VM traffic.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:22.700
And we can support any type
of connectivity design,

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:25.940
really making building
network topology and accessing

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:27.710
the service simplier.

00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:31.220
So now that we can connect
to all services easily,

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:33.870
we need a way to
manage them at scale.

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.170
So now I'm going
to hand off to Matt

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:40.632
to go through service
directory with you guys.

00:13:40.632 --> 00:13:41.840
MATT DELORIA: Thanks, Pierre.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:42.798
My name's Matt Deloria.

00:13:42.798 --> 00:13:45.320
I am the tech lead
of service directory.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:47.250
This is a product
that we were launch--

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:50.830
that we released to
public beta this spring.

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:54.070
And I'm really excited to
share with everyone here

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:55.810
what we've been doing
with this product

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:57.530
and how it fits
into Google Cloud.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.600
So with Service Directory,
what we were seeing

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.690
is that managing
services, as enterprises

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:08.790
adopt a more service-oriented
architecture,

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:11.890
becomes really
challenging to manage.

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:14.130
So what we're noticing
is that customers

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:19.080
are deploying services
to both not just on GCP,

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:23.340
but they still have some legacy
services that they keep on-prem

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:27.050
or, even within GCP, they
may be running services

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:28.300
across different environments.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:31.920
So a customer may not just
choose one particular product,

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:34.428
like GKE, they may choose
a handful of products,

00:14:34.428 --> 00:14:35.970
whether that's being
Cloud Functions,

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:42.610
Cloud Run, or just legacy
VMs or load balance services.

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:45.120
So it was really
challenging for customers

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:48.053
to manage their entire
service inventory.

00:14:48.053 --> 00:14:49.470
So we introduced
Service Directory

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:51.430
to help solve this problem.

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:55.310
So with Service Directory,
you can publish, discover,

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:56.810
and connect all
of your services,

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:58.970
regardless of their
environment or whether or not

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:04.660
they're running on GCP,
third-party cloud, or on-prem.

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:07.480
More importantly, it also
can scale to thousands

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:09.150
of services and endpoints.

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:13.720
It can serve real-time
information about your services

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:14.410
to any of your--

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:17.380
both your applications and
the users of your service,

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:20.320
whether that actually be the
teams that are deploying them

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:23.240
or organizations that
they're being shared with.

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:25.540
But you're like, what does
Service Directory really

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:27.220
enable?

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:30.100
And there's, I think,
three kind of core tenants

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:33.400
of what of Service Directory
enables for enterprises.

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:37.980
The first is that it's a way to
discover and publish services

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:39.280
from anywhere, right?

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:40.780
And this is a really
powerful thing.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:43.803
So no matter where
your services are,

00:15:43.803 --> 00:15:45.970
where they're running, where
their applications are,

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:48.387
you can always know you can
reach out to Service Directory

00:15:48.387 --> 00:15:49.735
to find them.

00:15:49.735 --> 00:15:51.110
So Service Directory
has a couple

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:56.353
of ways that this is really
leverages many of the features

00:15:56.353 --> 00:15:57.270
within GCP to do this.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:16:00.700
So Service Directory
has a DNS integration

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.230
where-- so if you change or
register any of your services

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:05.000
to Service Directory,
these services

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.820
become available over
DNS within seconds.

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:12.620
We also expose a really
rich HTP and gRPC API

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:16.370
that allows clients that
need to query using metadata

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:19.850
and things like that and be
able to do that without having

00:16:19.850 --> 00:16:23.020
to be on Google's network.

00:16:23.020 --> 00:16:25.810
Similarly, Search
Directory offers a way

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:28.100
to catalog your
service architecture.

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:29.830
And what we mean
here is that, so, we

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:31.330
have services that
are spread across

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:32.980
many different environments.

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:37.970
And this has challenges that,
where does a service live?

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:39.760
Where does a service live?

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:41.950
Where is-- like what
region is it in?

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:43.190
What type of service is it?

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:44.100
Is it a replica?

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:45.460
Is it a leader?

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:48.220
What kind of policies are
associated with the service

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:50.642
to make consumption
of services simple?

00:16:50.642 --> 00:16:53.540
So at Service Directory,
you have a single place

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:55.970
where you can then actually
attach attributes, attach

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:59.330
metadata, attach policies, where
you can enable all of this.

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:02.270
And you can do this all from
through a-- through our API,

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:07.260
or you can also do this through
Google Developer Console.

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:09.890
And the other important
thing about Service Directory

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:12.839
is that it's also a
way to share services.

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:17.435
So organizations, generally, or
an enterprise, their services

00:17:17.435 --> 00:17:19.810
may have to be shared, or they
may have to be restricted.

00:17:19.810 --> 00:17:22.530
So I may only want a specific
team within my organization

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:24.510
to be able to produce
our consumer service,

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:27.569
or I may have to share with
a third party, which I've

00:17:27.569 --> 00:17:29.860
[INAUDIBLE] into a partnership.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:31.770
At Service Directory,
that's really simple.

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:33.810
You can restrict services
with IAM policies

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:37.110
and B2C Service controls to be
ensured that the right people

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:40.190
are connected to your services.

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:43.420
And lastly, I think one of
the most important things

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:45.560
is that Service Directory
is completely managed.

00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:48.760
So a lot of ways enterprises
have been dealing

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:50.500
with this problem
today are either

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:54.820
by deploy their own software,
write their own software,

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:58.660
or use static files to kind
of register their services

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:01.960
or have to deploy
open-source plugins.

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:03.820
These can be really
challenging to run,

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:06.460
and this is actually a
really critical piece

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:07.390
of infrastructure.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:08.890
So what we want is
for customers not

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.880
have to worry about
running this at all.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:14.040
So with Service
Directory, so you

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:16.410
get a very simple
way and completely

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.390
managed way of
registering your services,

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:20.820
but also knowing that it's
integrated across our platform,

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:25.725
so you have integrations
with Stackdriver, our G Cloud

00:18:25.725 --> 00:18:30.450
and API libraries, and also,
of course, developer console.

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:37.240
So here's, really, a kind of
way that Service Directory can

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:39.680
be used in enterprises today.

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:42.520
So what I want to
really focus on here

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:47.310
is that there are really two
aspects to Service Directory.

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:48.980
On the right-hand
side, we have the ways

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:50.980
of things can be published
at Service Directory.

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:52.475
So it doesn't matter--

00:18:52.475 --> 00:18:54.850
if looking here, that doesn't
matter what type of service

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:58.510
it is, whether or not it's
GKE or multi-cluster GKE,

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:01.385
whether it's a Google Cloud
API, whether it's on-prem.

00:19:01.385 --> 00:19:03.010
All of these services
can be registered

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:05.290
in the Service Directory.

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:08.020
And a lot of these things
can be-- can be very simply

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:10.760
done just by, when you create
the resource, by actually

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:13.730
specifying the service
directory name.

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:16.380
And then from there,
Service Directory will--

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:19.920
if desired, can push
things to private zone DNS.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:24.732
And we also use IAM to
verify that both producers

00:19:24.732 --> 00:19:26.190
and the consumers
of these services

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:29.280
have the correct
permissions to do so.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:31.350
Now, on the consumer
side, you can

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:34.920
see that it doesn't matter which
type of application it is--

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:36.120
everybody can query--

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:37.210
[INAUDIBLE] to the API.

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.570
So if I'm running
on the VPC, I have

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:44.770
a way of using DNS to actually
look this up via DNS, via SRV,

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:46.500
or A records.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:51.090
Or I can use the HTP or gRPC API
if I'm both out of the network

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:52.585
or on our on-prem data site.

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:58.730
Last slide, so what are we
doing with Service Directory?

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:01.520
So as you saw in many of
the previous slides, some

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:04.880
of the integrations
that we are doing,

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:06.380
these will be-- we
would be starting

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:07.400
to relay out this year.

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:09.680
We're really
building integrations

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:11.840
with-- when you create an
internal load balancer,

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:12.770
they'll be
automatically registered

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:13.910
to Service Directory.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:16.010
Same with GKE services--

00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:18.770
we're also going to be adding
some open source tooling

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:21.230
to kind of make it really
simple for developers

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:23.390
to be able to publish into
Service Directory using

00:20:23.390 --> 00:20:26.280
the tooling that
they have today.

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:30.713
We're also now announcing
partnerships with-- we had one,

00:20:30.713 --> 00:20:32.130
recently, with
Cisco that has just

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:36.080
announced as part of
Google Cloud Next, where

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:39.990
Cisco's SD1 offering's going
to be using Service Directory's

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:44.040
ability to inform the
location of services.

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:48.750
And Cisco is using us to inform
network topologies and policies

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:52.880
of SD-WAN services.

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:54.635
And now for Private
Service Connect--

00:20:54.635 --> 00:20:57.260
so Service Directory is playing
a really-- a critical role here

00:20:57.260 --> 00:20:59.342
in the Private
Service Connect, where

00:20:59.342 --> 00:21:00.800
private services
will be registered

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:01.675
to Service Directory.

00:21:01.675 --> 00:21:04.100
And I'm going to allow Pierre
to talk a little bit more

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:05.597
about why this is
so important, how

00:21:05.597 --> 00:21:07.805
this is a very different
way of approaching services.

00:21:09.953 --> 00:21:11.120
PIERRE ETTORI: Thanks, Matt.

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:13.790
So Private Service Connect
and Service Directory

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:17.030
combined together
really were hand-in-hand

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:20.650
and provide a better together
story where access to service

00:21:20.650 --> 00:21:23.270
is now easy,
secure, and private,

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:24.740
no matter if the
traffic is coming

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:27.090
from on-prem or in the cloud.

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:29.660
And as companies connect
to more and more services

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:32.420
across Google Services,
third-party vendor,

00:21:32.420 --> 00:21:35.630
and their own services,
Service Directory really

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:38.420
helps ensuring team
to manage publishing

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:41.630
and discovering services,
allowing technology team

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:45.850
to deliver services faster
and more securely at scale.

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:49.110
So really see Private Service
Connect and Service Directory

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:52.640
as kind of like foundation for
the service-centric networking

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:54.840
solution that
Google is offering.

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:57.880
And we are excited to
have you guys try too.

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:00.500
And with that, I would
like two thank Matt

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:02.502
for presenting with me today.

00:22:02.502 --> 00:22:03.710
MATT DELORIA: Thanks, Pierre.

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:05.750
Thanks everyone today
for learning more

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:07.660
about Service Directory.

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:09.410
PIERRE ETTORI: Thanks,
all, for your time.

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:11.910
I hope you learned a lot today
about Private Service Connect

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:13.630
and Service Directory.

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:17.280
We're excited for you guys
to start testing our product.

